 plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-2,2,1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,2) & =(-2)^{2}(2)+2(-2)-(2) \\\\  & =8-4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-2,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-1 & =2(y-2) \\\\ z & =2y-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=2y-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406791 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186406799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406791 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186406800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406791 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186406801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406791 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186406802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406791 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406791 #edb/ref 1045 "Geometric Interpretations of Partial Derivatives" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406791 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186406800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406791 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406791 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406791 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "fa97339f-4dd8-5f3c-87ec-f76f876eeb93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406792 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨1,0,2⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406792 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406792 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "047539be-1b12-5b55-a1be-3e4cf9e127fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406793 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨1,0,1⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406793 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406793 #edb/ref 1083 #uui